  "desc": "Built to Disappear",
  "inspiration": "https://kohlmayer-oberst-architekten.de/projekte/\n\nhttps://www.thisispaper.com/mag/house-djh2o-kohlmayer-oberst\n\nEx Machina (Alex Garland, 2014), filmed at the Juvet Landscape Hotel near Ålesund, Norway\n\nThe white photo room at Kojima Productions, Tokyo, where Hideo Kojima photographs his guests",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "head": false,
      "html": "<span style=\"caret-color: rgb(0, 0, 0); color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: -webkit-standard; font-size: medium; letter-spacing: normal;\">Some houses feel like they were built to disappear. This one, completed in 2004 on the banks of the Enz in Bietigheim, Germany, calls to mind&nbsp;</span><em style=\"caret-color: rgb(0, 0, 0); color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-size: medium; letter-spacing: normal;\">Ex Machina</em><span style=\"caret-color: rgb(0, 0, 0); color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-family: -webkit-standard; font-size: medium; letter-spacing: normal;\">, shot at the Juvet Landscape Hotel near Ålesund, and the white room at Kojima Productions in Tokyo, where Hideo Kojima photographs the guests who visit his studio: glowing ceiling, glass walls, a floor that mirrors everything. There’s something serene and raw about it, as if it exists only to complement the nature around it, the person living in it, and what they create.</span><br>"
    },
